IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `mca-nouns`

-- Minors' Contracts Act 1987 (Singapore), 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation in force from
-- 1 April 2022 -- LAYER 1: THE LAW, sections 1 to 4 in the Act's own order (section 5, the short title,
-- is machinery and is not encoded; see NOTES.md).  Written from the text alone; it takes nobody's side.
--
-- HOW TO READ A RESULT (NOTES.md, "Reading a result: FALSE and NOTHING").  Every decision function answers a
-- MAYBE BOOLEAN.  JUST FALSE means the Act itself says no on these facts, in its own words.  NOTHING
-- means the Act does not answer on these facts; it never means no.  JUST TRUE means the Act's own words
-- give the answer yes.  Each branch that answers JUST FALSE carries a FALSE-kind comment quoting the
-- words, and each that answers NOTHING carries a NOTHING-kind comment saying why.
--
-- NAMES.  A function is named for what the section DOES, so that its JUST FALSE cannot be read as a claim
-- about something the section does not say ("the section does not save the guarantee", never "the
-- guarantee is unenforceable").
--
-- SECTION 4 IS A GATE ON SECTIONS 1 TO 3 ("Nothing in this Act applies in relation to a contract made after
-- 9 April 1987 and before 12 November 1993 except ...").  It is written last, as the Act orders it, and read
-- by the three sections above it; L4 does not care about the order of definitions.  Each of sections 1 to 3
-- asks the same question of it, in the same order: (1) section 4 shuts the provision out -> JUST FALSE; (2) the
-- section's own "where" fails -> JUST FALSE, or its own scope limit fails -> NOTHING; (3) section 4 leaves open
-- whether its exception is met -> NOTHING; (4) otherwise (s 4 does not speak, or its exception is met) the
-- section's own words answer.

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§ `Section 1 -- Disapplication of Infants Relief Act 1874`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- src:32 | 1. The Infants Relief Act 1874 (which invalidates certain contracts
-- src:34 | made by minors and prohibits actions to enforce contracts ratified
-- src:35 | after majority) shall not apply to any contract made by a minor after
-- src:36 | 9 April 1987.
-- The parenthesis of s 1 describes the Infants Relief Act 1874 by two effects.  "Certain contracts" is the
-- Act's own vague phrase: which contracts the 1874 Act invalidated is not stated in these sources.
@ref s 1 (src:32-36)
DECLARE `Effect of the Infants Relief Act 1874` IS ONE OF
    `invalidating certain contracts made by minors`
    `prohibiting actions to enforce contracts ratified after majority`

@ref s 1 (src:32-35)
GIVETH A LIST OF `Effect of the Infants Relief Act 1874`
`s 1 the effects the Act describes the Infants Relief Act 1874 as having` MEANS
    LIST
        `invalidating certain contracts made by minors`,
        `prohibiting actions to enforce contracts ratified after majority`

@ref s 1 (src:35-36)
GIVEN contract IS A `Contract with a minor`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
-- The scope of s 1: "any contract made by a minor after 9 April 1987".
`s 1 the contract was made by a minor after 9 April 1987` MEANS
        contract's `one party was a minor when the contract was made`
    AND `the day is after 9 April 1987` (contract's `day the contract was made`)

@ref s 1 (src:32-36)
GIVEN contract IS A `Contract with a minor`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 1 the disapplication reaches the contract` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `s 4 the provision of this Act is open to apply to the contract` contract EQUALS JUST FALSE
            THEN JUST FALSE
            -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Nothing in this Act applies in relation to a contract made after 9 April 1987 and before 12 November 1993" (s 4, read as a gate on this section)
        IF NOT (`the day is after 9 April 1987` (contract's `day the contract was made`))
            THEN JUST FALSE
            -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "any contract made by a minor after 9 April 1987" -- the date is fixed in the text's own words, and a contract made on or before 9 April 1987 is outside it: s 1 does not disapply the 1874 Act for it (rulings of 2026-11-05, periods (a) and "where" (b); fork F2)
        IF NOT (`s 1 the contract was made by a minor after 9 April 1987` contract)
            THEN NOTHING
            -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): once the date is met, s 1 reaches "any contract made by a minor" and says nothing of a contract made by someone who was not a minor (a scope limit with no "only": ruling of 2026-11-05 (c); fork F2)
        IF `s 4 the provision of this Act is open to apply to the contract` contract EQUALS NOTHING
            THEN NOTHING
            -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): whether s 1 survives s 4 turns on "section 5 of the Civil Law Act 1909", which is not among the sources, and the person does not know
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

@ref s 1 (src:32-36)
GIVEN contract IS A `Contract with a minor`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 1 the Infants Relief Act 1874 applies to the contract` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 1 the disapplication reaches the contract` contract
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST FALSE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "The Infants Relief Act 1874 ... shall not apply to any contract made by a minor after 9 April 1987"
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): s 1 does not reach this contract (s 4 shuts it out, or it was made on or before 9 April 1987), and the Act does not say whether the 1874 Act applies to it
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): s 1 neither reaches nor misses this contract on these facts, and the Act does not say whether the 1874 Act applies to it

@ref s 1 "which invalidates certain contracts made by minors" (src:32-35)
GIVEN contract IS A `Contract with a minor`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 1 the Infants Relief Act 1874 invalidates the contract` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 1 the Infants Relief Act 1874 applies to the contract` contract
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "The Infants Relief Act 1874 (which invalidates certain contracts made by minors ...) shall not apply"
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): the text never says the 1874 Act applies; and which contracts are "certain contracts" is not stated (unreachable on this text, kept so that a change to the rule above cannot silently answer yes)
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the Act does not say whether the 1874 Act applies, so it does not say whether that Act invalidates this contract

@ref s 1 "prohibits actions to enforce contracts ratified after majority" (src:34-35)
GIVEN contract IS A `Contract with a minor`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 1 the Infants Relief Act 1874 bars an action on the contract ratified after majority` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 1 the Infants Relief Act 1874 applies to the contract` contract
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "The Infants Relief Act 1874 (which ... prohibits actions to enforce contracts ratified after majority) shall not apply"
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the text never says the 1874 Act applies (unreachable on this text, kept so that a change to the rule above cannot silently answer yes)
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the Act does not say whether the 1874 Act applies, so it does not say whether that Act bars an action on this contract

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§ `Section 2 -- Guarantees`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- src:39 | 2. Where —
-- src:40 | (a) a guarantee is given in respect of an obligation of a party to
-- src:41 | a contract made after 9 April 1987; and
@ref s 2(a) (src:39-41)
GIVEN gc IS A `Guarantee case`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
-- A limb test, not a decision: FALSE only means the limb is not met.
`s 2(a) a guarantee is given in respect of an obligation of a party to a contract made after 9 April 1987` MEANS
        gc's `a guarantee is given in respect of an obligation of a party to the contract`
    AND `the day is after 9 April 1987` (gc's `the contract guaranteed`'s `day the contract was made`)

-- src:42 | (b) the obligation is unenforceable against him (or he
-- src:43 | repudiates the contract) because he was a minor when
-- src:44 | the contract was made,
@ref s 2(b) (src:42-44)
GIVEN contract IS A `Contract with a minor`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
-- A limb test.  JUST FALSE = the limb is not met; NOTHING = the person does not know a fact the limb turns on
-- (that the obligation is unenforceable, or that this is because of minority, is law outside the sources).
-- The record's own fact about minority is read first: when no party was a minor when the contract was made,
-- "because he was a minor" cannot hold, whatever else is known.
-- FORK F1: "because he was a minor ..." is read as governing BOTH limbs, "the obligation is unenforceable
-- against him" and "(or he repudiates the contract)".  The other reading (repudiation for any reason) would
-- be: (unenforceable AND because) OR repudiates.  The Act's "for that reason alone" in the tail of s 2 refers
-- back to minority, which is why the first reading is taken.  Shared by s 3(1)(b), whose words are the same.
`s 2(b) the obligation is unenforceable against him, or he repudiates the contract, because he was a minor when the contract was made` MEANS
    IF NOT (contract's `one party was a minor when the contract was made`)
    THEN JUST FALSE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "because he was a minor when the contract was made" -- the record says that no party was a minor when the contract was made, so the condition cannot be met (a "where" that fails: ruling of 2026-11-05 (b); fork F1)
    ELSE CONSIDER contract's `that is because that party was a minor when the contract was made`
        WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE
            -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "because he was a minor when the contract was made" -- the reason given is not minority (a "where" that fails: ruling of 2026-11-05 (b); fork F1)
        WHEN NOTHING THEN
            IF (NOT (contract's `that party repudiates the contract`)) AND (contract's `the obligation is unenforceable against that party` EQUALS JUST FALSE)
            THEN JUST FALSE
            ELSE NOTHING
        WHEN JUST TRUE THEN
            IF contract's `that party repudiates the contract`
            THEN JUST TRUE
            ELSE contract's `the obligation is unenforceable against that party`

-- src:39 | 2. Where —
-- src:40 | (a) a guarantee is given in respect of an obligation of a party to
-- src:41 | a contract made after 9 April 1987; and
-- src:42 | (b) the obligation is unenforceable against him (or he
-- src:43 | repudiates the contract) because he was a minor when
-- src:44 | the contract was made,
-- src:46 | the guarantee shall not for that reason alone be unenforceable against
-- src:47 | the guarantor.
@ref s 2 (src:39-47)
GIVEN gc IS A `Guarantee case`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 2 the guarantee is saved from unenforceability on that ground alone` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `s 4 the provision of this Act is open to apply to the contract` (gc's `the contract guaranteed`) EQUALS JUST FALSE
            THEN JUST FALSE
            -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Nothing in this Act applies in relation to a contract made after 9 April 1987 and before 12 November 1993" (s 4, read as a gate on this section)
        IF NOT (`s 2(a) a guarantee is given in respect of an obligation of a party to a contract made after 9 April 1987` gc)
            THEN JUST FALSE
            -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Where — (a) a guarantee is given in respect of an obligation of a party to a contract made after 9 April 1987" -- a "where" that fails (ruling of 2026-11-05 (b); reading (a) of the rule of 2026-10-29): this section does not save the guarantee on these facts
        IF `s 2(b) the obligation is unenforceable against him, or he repudiates the contract, because he was a minor when the contract was made` (gc's `the contract guaranteed`) EQUALS JUST FALSE
            THEN JUST FALSE
            -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "(b) the obligation is unenforceable against him (or he repudiates the contract) because he was a minor when the contract was made" -- a "where" that fails (ruling of 2026-11-05 (b)): this section does not save the guarantee on these facts
        IF `s 2(b) the obligation is unenforceable against him, or he repudiates the contract, because he was a minor when the contract was made` (gc's `the contract guaranteed`) EQUALS NOTHING
            THEN NOTHING
            -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): whether the obligation is unenforceable against a minor, and because of his minority, is law outside the sources, and the person does not know
        IF `s 4 the provision of this Act is open to apply to the contract` (gc's `the contract guaranteed`) EQUALS NOTHING
            THEN NOTHING
            -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): whether s 2 survives s 4 turns on "section 5 of the Civil Law Act 1909", which is not among the sources, and the person does not know
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

@ref s 2 (src:46-47)
GIVEN gc IS A `Guarantee case`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 2 the guarantee is unenforceable against the guarantor for that reason alone` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 2 the guarantee is saved from unenforceability on that ground alone` gc
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST FALSE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "the guarantee shall not for that reason alone be unenforceable against the guarantor"
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): s 2 speaks only "where" (a) and (b) hold and s 4 does not shut it out; when they do not, it does not say the guarantee is unenforceable, nor that it is enforceable
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): whether s 2 stands for this contract depends on section 5 of the Civil Law Act 1909, which is not among the sources

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§ `Section 3 -- Restitution`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- src:51 | (a) a person (the claimant) has after 9 April 1987 entered into a
-- src:52 | contract with another (the defendant); and
@ref s 3(1)(a) "the claimant"; "the defendant" (src:51-52)
GIVETH A LIST OF `Defined term in the Act`
`s 3(1)(a) the terms the Act defines` MEANS
    LIST
        `Defined term in the Act` OF
            "the claimant"
            "s 3(1)(a)"
            "a person (the claimant) has after 9 April 1987 entered into a contract with another (the defendant)"
            "the person who has, after 9 April 1987, entered into a contract with another"
        `Defined term in the Act` OF
            "the defendant"
            "s 3(1)(a)"
            "a person (the claimant) has after 9 April 1987 entered into a contract with another (the defendant)"
            "the other party to that contract; s 3(1)(b) goes on to say that he was a minor when it was made"

@ref s 3(1)(a) (src:51-52)
GIVEN word IS A STRING
GIVETH A MAYBE `Defined term in the Act`
`s 3(1)(a) the Act's definition of` word MEANS
    CONSIDER filter (GIVEN t YIELD t's term EQUALS word) `s 3(1)(a) the terms the Act defines`
    WHEN EMPTY THEN NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): the Act defines only "the claimant" and "the defendant"; any other word, "minor", "guarantee" and "property" included, is left to be asked as a fact
    WHEN t FOLLOWED BY rest THEN JUST t

-- src:50 | 3.—(1) Where —
-- src:51 | (a) a person (the claimant) has after 9 April 1987 entered into a
-- src:52 | contract with another (the defendant); and
@ref s 3(1)(a) (src:50-52)
GIVEN rc IS A `Restitution case`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
-- A limb test, not a decision: FALSE only means the limb is not met.
`s 3(1)(a) the claimant has after 9 April 1987 entered into a contract with the defendant` MEANS
        rc's `the claimant entered into the contract with the defendant`
    AND `the day is after 9 April 1987` (rc's `the contract sued on`'s `day the contract was made`)

-- src:59 | (b) the contract is unenforceable against the defendant (or he
-- src:60 | repudiates it) because he was a minor when the contract
-- src:61 | was made,
@ref s 3(1)(b) (src:59-61)
GIVEN contract IS A `Contract with a minor`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
-- A limb test (JUST FALSE = not met; NOTHING = not known).  Its words are the words of s 2(b), so it is the same test (fork F1).
`s 3(1)(b) the contract is unenforceable against the defendant, or he repudiates it, because he was a minor when the contract was made` MEANS
    `s 2(b) the obligation is unenforceable against him, or he repudiates the contract, because he was a minor when the contract was made` contract

-- src:50 | 3.—(1) Where —
-- src:51 | (a) a person (the claimant) has after 9 April 1987 entered into a
-- src:52 | contract with another (the defendant); and
-- src:59 | (b) the contract is unenforceable against the defendant (or he
-- src:60 | repudiates it) because he was a minor when the contract
-- src:61 | was made,
@ref s 3(1) (src:50-61)
GIVEN rc IS A `Restitution case`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 3(1) the conditions for the court's power are met` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `s 4 the provision of this Act is open to apply to the contract` (rc's `the contract sued on`) EQUALS JUST FALSE
            THEN JUST FALSE
            -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Nothing in this Act applies in relation to a contract made after 9 April 1987 and before 12 November 1993" (s 4, read as a gate on this section)
        IF NOT (`s 3(1)(a) the claimant has after 9 April 1987 entered into a contract with the defendant` rc)
            THEN JUST FALSE
            -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Where — (a) a person (the claimant) has after 9 April 1987 entered into a contract with another (the defendant)" -- a "where" that fails (ruling of 2026-11-05 (b)): the court has no power under this section on these facts
        IF `s 3(1)(b) the contract is unenforceable against the defendant, or he repudiates it, because he was a minor when the contract was made` (rc's `the contract sued on`) EQUALS JUST FALSE
            THEN JUST FALSE
            -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "(b) the contract is unenforceable against the defendant (or he repudiates it) because he was a minor when the contract was made" -- a "where" that fails (ruling of 2026-11-05 (b)): the court has no power under this section on these facts
        IF `s 3(1)(b) the contract is unenforceable against the defendant, or he repudiates it, because he was a minor when the contract was made` (rc's `the contract sued on`) EQUALS NOTHING
            THEN NOTHING
            -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): whether the contract is unenforceable against a minor, and because of his minority, is law outside the sources, and the person does not know
        IF `s 4 the provision of this Act is open to apply to the contract` (rc's `the contract sued on`) EQUALS NOTHING
            THEN NOTHING
            -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): whether s 3 survives s 4 turns on "section 5 of the Civil Law Act 1909", which is not among the sources, and the person does not know
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- src:63 | the court may, if it is just and equitable to do so, require the defendant
-- src:64 | to transfer to the claimant any property acquired by the defendant
-- src:65 | under the contract, or any property representing it.
@ref s 3(1) "to the claimant any property acquired by the defendant under the contract, or any property representing it" (src:63-65)
GIVEN property IS A Property
      transferee IS A Transferee
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
-- "It" is read as the property acquired (fork F4); "any property representing it" is property that stands in
-- the place of the property acquired, so the field says so.
`s 3(1) the property is within what the court may require to be transferred` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF transferee EQUALS `someone other than the claimant`
            THEN NOTHING
            -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the court may require the defendant "to transfer to the claimant"; a transfer to anyone else is not given
        IF property's `acquired by the defendant under the contract`
            THEN JUST TRUE
        IF property's `represents property acquired by the defendant under the contract`
            THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE NOTHING
            -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the grant is of "any property acquired by the defendant under the contract, or any property representing it"; other property is not given (and not refused)

-- The court's power: the where-conditions, the judgment "if it is just and equitable to do so" (the court's
-- own judgment, taken as a plain input; the person states what they or the court think, or NOTHING), and the
-- property.  JUST TRUE means the court HAS the power ("may"); it does not say the court will use it.
@ref s 3(1) (src:63-65)
GIVEN rc IS A `Restitution case`
      property IS A Property
      transferee IS A Transferee
      `just and equitable` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 3(1) the court may require the defendant to transfer property to the claimant` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 3(1) the conditions for the court's power are met` rc
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): the "where" of s 3(1) is not met (or s 4 shuts s 3 out): "Where — (a) ...; and (b) ..." -- no power on these facts
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): whether s 3 stands for this contract depends on section 5 of the Civil Law Act 1909, which is not among the sources
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN
        CONSIDER `just and equitable`
        WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE
            -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "if it is just and equitable to do so" -- an "if" that fails (reading (a) of the rule of 2026-10-29)
        WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
            -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): "just and equitable" is a judgment the Act hands to the court and states no test for
        WHEN JUST TRUE THEN
            `s 3(1) the property is within what the court may require to be transferred` property transferee

-- The same power as a permission of the court (a MAY with no deadline: the Act states none).  A court that
-- does not use it is not in breach: an unused MAY is FULFILLED.  Run with #TRACE in mca-cases.l4; a deontic
-- cannot be compared in #ASSERT on this binary, so the decision function above is what is asserted.
@ref s 3(1) (src:63-65)
GIVEN rc IS A `Restitution case`
      property IS A Property
      transferee IS A Transferee
      `just and equitable` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Party under section 3` `Act under section 3`
`s 3(1) the court's permission to require the transfer` MEANS
    IF `s 3(1) the court may require the defendant to transfer property to the claimant` rc property transferee `just and equitable` EQUALS JUST TRUE
    THEN PARTY `the court`
         MAY `require the defendant to transfer property to the claimant`
         HENCE FULFILLED
    ELSE FULFILLED

-- src:69 | (2) Nothing in this section shall be taken to prejudice any other
-- src:70 | remedy available to the claimant.
@ref s 3(2) (src:69-70)
GIVEN rc IS A `Restitution case`
      remedy IS A Remedy
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
-- FORK F5: "the claimant" is the label s 3(1)(a) gives; s 3(2) is read to need only limb (a), not limb (b).
-- JUST FALSE answers "does this section prejudice the remedy?": no.  It is the text's flat negative.
`s 3(2) this section prejudices another remedy available to the claimant` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `s 4 the provision of this Act is open to apply to the contract` (rc's `the contract sued on`) EQUALS JUST FALSE
            THEN JUST FALSE
            -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Nothing in this Act applies in relation to a contract made after 9 April 1987 and before 12 November 1993" (s 4): this section does nothing to any remedy
        IF NOT (`s 3(1)(a) the claimant has after 9 April 1987 entered into a contract with the defendant` rc)
            THEN NOTHING
            -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): s 3(2) speaks of "the claimant", the label s 3(1)(a) gives; for anyone else it says nothing
        IF NOT (remedy's `other than a transfer under section 3(1)` AND remedy's `available to the claimant`)
            THEN NOTHING
            -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): s 3(2) protects "any other remedy available to the claimant"; a remedy that is the s 3(1) transfer itself, or is not available to the claimant, is outside that wording
        IF `s 4 the provision of this Act is open to apply to the contract` (rc's `the contract sued on`) EQUALS NOTHING
            THEN NOTHING
            -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): whether s 3 survives s 4 turns on "section 5 of the Civil Law Act 1909", which is not among the sources, and the person does not know
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE
            -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Nothing in this section shall be taken to prejudice any other remedy available to the claimant"

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§ `Section 4 -- Contracts before 12 November 1993`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- src:76 | 4. Nothing in this Act applies in relation to a contract made after
-- src:77 | 9 April 1987 and before 12 November 1993 except in so far as it was
-- src:78 | applicable by virtue of section 5 of the Civil Law Act 1909 in force
-- src:79 | before 12 November 1993.
@ref s 4 "9 April 1987" (src:76-79)
GIVETH A DATE
`9 April 1987` MEANS Date 9 4 1987

@ref s 4 "12 November 1993" (src:77-79)
GIVETH A DATE
`12 November 1993` MEANS Date 12 11 1993

-- The day test the Act uses in four places: s 1 "made by a minor after 9 April 1987", s 2(a) "a contract made after
-- 9 April 1987", s 3(1)(a) "has after 9 April 1987 entered into a contract" and s 4 "made after 9 April 1987".  It is
-- a neutral helper (so its name carries no one section) and a limb test, not a decision: FALSE only means the
-- day is on or before 9 April 1987.  Each section reads it in its own way (see FORK F2).
@ref s 4 "after 9 April 1987" (src:76-77); read also by s 1, s 2(a) and s 3(1)(a)
GIVEN day IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the day is after 9 April 1987` MEANS
    day GREATER THAN `9 April 1987`

-- The three bands the dates of s 4 make.  "After 9 April 1987" and "before 12 November 1993" are both strict.
DECLARE `Band under section 4` IS ONE OF
    `made on or before 9 April 1987`
    `made after 9 April 1987 and before 12 November 1993`
    `made on or after 12 November 1993`

@ref s 4 (src:76-77)
GIVEN day IS A DATE
GIVETH A `Band under section 4`
`s 4 the band a contract made on a day falls in` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF day AT MOST `9 April 1987`
            THEN `made on or before 9 April 1987`
        IF day LESS THAN `12 November 1993`
            THEN `made after 9 April 1987 and before 12 November 1993`
        OTHERWISE `made on or after 12 November 1993`

-- What s 4 does to a provision of the Act, for one contract.  A typed representation of the four things the
-- section can do, so that sections 1 to 3 can ask it once.
DECLARE `What section 4 does to a provision of this Act` IS ONE OF
    `s 4 does not speak to this contract`
    `s 4 shuts this provision out`
    `s 4 leaves this provision to section 5 of the Civil Law Act 1909`
    `s 4 leaves open whether section 5 of the Civil Law Act 1909 made this provision applicable`

@ref s 4 (src:76-79)
GIVEN contract IS A `Contract with a minor`
GIVETH A `What section 4 does to a provision of this Act`
`s 4 what this section does to a provision of this Act` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 4 the band a contract made on a day falls in` (contract's `day the contract was made`)
    WHEN `made after 9 April 1987 and before 12 November 1993` THEN
        CONSIDER contract's `section 5 of the Civil Law Act 1909 made the provision applicable`
        WHEN JUST b THEN
            IF b
            THEN `s 4 leaves this provision to section 5 of the Civil Law Act 1909`
            ELSE `s 4 shuts this provision out`
        WHEN NOTHING THEN `s 4 leaves open whether section 5 of the Civil Law Act 1909 made this provision applicable`
    OTHERWISE `s 4 does not speak to this contract`

-- Used by sections 1 to 3 as their gate.  JUST FALSE = s 4 shuts the provision out; NOTHING = s 4 leaves open
-- whether the exception is met (a rule outside the sources decides it, and the person does not know);
-- JUST TRUE = s 4 does not speak to the contract, or the exception is met so that the provision goes on applying
-- (QA (NOTES.md, Judgment calls): the other reading is NOTHING for the second, "a lifted bar grants nothing").
@ref s 4 (src:76-79)
GIVEN contract IS A `Contract with a minor`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 4 the provision of this Act is open to apply to the contract` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 4 what this section does to a provision of this Act` contract
    WHEN `s 4 shuts this provision out` THEN JUST FALSE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Nothing in this Act applies in relation to a contract made after 9 April 1987 and before 12 November 1993"
    WHEN `s 4 leaves this provision to section 5 of the Civil Law Act 1909` THEN JUST TRUE
        -- QA (NOTES.md, Judgment calls): the exception is met ("except in so far as it was applicable by virtue of section 5 of the Civil Law Act 1909"), so the bar does not operate and the provision's own words answer. The bar question `s 4 this Act is barred from applying to the contract` answers JUST FALSE on the same facts, so the two functions agree, and they now agree outside the window as well (gate JUST TRUE, bar JUST FALSE). The other reading is NOTHING (a lifted bar grants nothing).
    WHEN `s 4 leaves open whether section 5 of the Civil Law Act 1909 made this provision applicable` THEN NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): whether the exception is met turns on section 5 of the Civil Law Act 1909, which is not among the sources, and the person does not know
    WHEN `s 4 does not speak to this contract` THEN JUST TRUE

-- The bar as a question of its own: "is this Act shut out of my contract?"  JUST TRUE is the text's own bar;
-- JUST FALSE is the exception met ("except in so far as ..."), which stops the bar, or a contract made outside
-- the window the text names, which the bar does not reach; NOTHING is a fact not known.
@ref s 4 (src:76-79)
GIVEN contract IS A `Contract with a minor`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 4 this Act is barred from applying to the contract` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 4 what this section does to a provision of this Act` contract
    WHEN `s 4 shuts this provision out` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 4 leaves this provision to section 5 of the Civil Law Act 1909` THEN JUST FALSE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "except in so far as it was applicable by virtue of section 5 of the Civil Law Act 1909 in force before 12 November 1993" -- the exception is met, so the bar does not operate to that extent
    WHEN `s 4 leaves open whether section 5 of the Civil Law Act 1909 made this provision applicable` THEN NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): whether the exception is met turns on section 5 of the Civil Law Act 1909, which is not among the sources, and the person does not know
    WHEN `s 4 does not speak to this contract` THEN JUST FALSE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "a contract made after 9 April 1987 and before 12 November 1993" -- the window is fixed in the text's own words, and a contract made on or before 9 April 1987, or on or after 12 November 1993, is outside it: s 4 does not bar the Act for it (rulings of 2026-11-05, periods (a) and the "until"-bar rule (d); fork F2)
